<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ephor</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as ephors</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ephor was the title of senior magistrates, best known from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, who supervised the conduct of kings and officials. Greek writers, notably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>, compared certain Carthaginian institutions to the Spartan ephors when describing the constitution of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punic-empire">Punic Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ephor.jpg" alt="Ephor" width="320" height="225" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ludwig Löffler. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sparta_Ephoren_(WMH_11-1861-62_S_48_LLoeffler).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word ephoros means one who oversees. The ephors were a board of five magistrates of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, elected each year, with judicial, religious, legislative and military powers. Ancient sources gave two accounts of their origin. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>, followed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophon">Xenophon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isocrates">Isocrates</a>, traced the office to the lawgiver Lycurgus. Aristotle said the king Theopompos created it, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> followed him. Modern scholars think the second account derives from a lost work by the exiled king Pausanias, who may have disliked the ephors, and most place the ephorate in Theopompos's time, during the Messenian Wars. Yurij Andreev points out that ancient Thera also had ephors, and suggests that the office existed earlier in some form.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>All Spartan citizens could be elected, and the office was the only one open to every man between 30 and 60 years of age. Ephors could not be re-elected. Elections took place near the autumn equinox, when the Spartan year began: candidates passed before the assembly, and a jury shut in a building judged who was greeted with the loudest shouts. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> called this method childish. One ephor, the eponymous ephor, gave his name to the year, and the Spartans kept a list of them.</p><p>Ephors presided over the assembly, acted as judges in the supreme civil court and handled foreign relations and treaties. Two accompanied a king on campaign and could put him on trial. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> they declared war on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helots">helots</a> each autumn, so that any Spartan could kill one without guilt, and they watched the sky every eight years to judge whether a king had offended the gods. They supervised the education of Spartan boys and chaired the congress of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-league">Peloponnesian League</a>. Aristotle observed that, since ephors often came from poor families, they were liable to corruption, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> called them tyrants.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Policy could change when a single ephor changed his vote; in 403 BCE Pausanias persuaded three ephors to send an army to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attica">Attica</a>, reversing the policy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysander">Lysander</a>. Cleomenes III abolished the office in 227 BCE, and four of the five ephors died in his coup, but after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sellasia">Battle of Sellasia</a> in 222 BCE the Macedonian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-iii-doson">Antigonus III Doson</a> restored it. The ephorate lasted under Roman rule into the second century CE, when Hadrian probably abolished it. Several cities of Spartan ancestry, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarentum">Taras</a>, Thera and Cyrene, also had ephors.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ephor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It could punish unsuccessful commanders severely and even crucify them, a sanction that encouraged effectiveness but may have discouraged strategic risk-taking; Aristotle likened this body to the Spartan <b>ephors</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/punic-empire">Punic Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Each year the <b>ephors</b> formally declared war on the helots, and the krypteia, a body of young men, could kill helots without penalty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sparta">Sparta · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>By Herodotus’ time their judicial role covered only heiresses, adoptions and the public roads, and the five <b>ephors</b>, elected every year, watched over them; two ephors went with a king on campaign.</blockquote><a href="/societies/sparta">Social Hierarchy of Ancient Sparta</a></li></ul></section>
